Subject: Input: soc_button_array: defer MSHW0040 probe until its GPIOs exist

The generic button path ignores -EPROBE_DEFER, so when soc_button_array
probes before the Panther Lake pinctrl driver, MSHW0040 binds with no
buttons (or stays unbound) and the power and volume keys never work.
MSHW0040 has a real power-button GPIO at index 0, so look it up in the
device check and defer until its provider is ready.

From zR-JB in linux-surface/linux-surface#2144.
---
--- a/drivers/input/misc/soc_button_array.c
+++ b/drivers/input/misc/soc_button_array.c
@@ -554,6 +554,7 @@ static int soc_device_check_MSHW0040(struct device *dev)
 	acpi_handle handle = ACPI_HANDLE(dev);
 	union acpi_object *result;
 	u64 oem_platform_rev = 0;	// valid revisions are nonzero
+	int gpio, irq, error;
 
 	// get OEM platform revision
 	result = acpi_evaluate_dsm_typed(handle, &MSHW0040_DSM_UUID,
@@ -576,6 +577,15 @@ static int soc_device_check_MSHW0040(struct device *dev)
 
 	dev_dbg(dev, "OEM Platform Revision %llu\n", oem_platform_rev);
 
+	/*
+	 * The generic button path ignores -EPROBE_DEFER because some platforms
+	 * expose virtual GPIOs that never get a provider. MSHW0040 has a real
+	 * power-button GPIO at index 0, so wait for its provider here.
+	 */
+	error = soc_button_lookup_gpio(dev, 0, &gpio, &irq);
+	if (error == -EPROBE_DEFER)
+		return error;
+
 	return 0;
 }
 
